<!-- Use cases -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="section-label">Use cases</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Built for the moments that matter</h2>
    <p class="section-sub">owit is not a dashboard replacement. It's what you reach for when something is broken and you need answers fast.</p>
    <div class="usecases-grid">
      <div class="usecase-card">
        <div class="usecase-scenario">Production incident</div>
        <div class="usecase-title">Everything in one place during an outage</div>
        <div class="usecase-desc">
          Your payment service is throwing 500s at 3am. Logs are in Loki, traces in Tempo, metrics in Datadog.
          Instead of switching between three browser tabs, one <span class="inline-code">owit</span> command returns everything correlated by trace ID — in your terminal, in seconds.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="usecase-card">
        <div class="usecase-scenario">Multi-vendor teams</div>
        <div class="usecase-title">One query language across every squad</div>
        <div class="usecase-desc">
          Platform chose Prometheus. Product chose Datadog. SRE chose CloudWatch.
          owit gives every engineer the same query syntax regardless of which vendor is behind it — no re-learning, no tribal knowledge.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="usecase-card">
        <div class="usecase-scenario">Terminal-first workflows</div>
        <div class="usecase-title">Pipe observability data like any other tool</div>
        <div class="usecase-desc">
          Dashboards are great for monitoring. Terminals are better for debugging.
          owit lives where you already work — pipe results to <span class="inline-code">grep</span>, <span class="inline-code">jq</span>, or your own scripts without ever opening a browser.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- How it works -->
<section class="section section-alt">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="section-label">How it works</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">From one command to every backend</h2>
    <p class="section-sub">Three commands and you're querying every backend at once. No config files to edit, no plugins to hunt down manually.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div>
          <div class="step-title">Install owit</div>
          <div class="step-desc">One command gets you the CLI. That's it.</div>
          <span class="step-code">owit install</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div>
          <div class="step-title">Connect your backends</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Tell owit where your backends are. It downloads the right plugin, stores the connection, and you never touch a config file.</div>
          <span class="step-code">owit backend add loki --url https://loki.internal --token $LOKI_TOKEN</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div>
          <div class="step-title">Query everything at once</div>
          <div class="step-desc">One command fans out to every connected backend in parallel. Filters read like plain English — no new syntax to memorise.</div>
          <span class="step-code">owit logs level=error service=api --last 1h --limit 25</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div>
          <div class="step-title">One result, every source</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Results from all backends arrive merged and sorted by timestamp. If a backend is down, you get an explicit warning alongside partial results — never silent data loss.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

